<div class="article-body">

<p>If you are shopping for the best cucumber varieties for indoor growing, there is one word to check, and it is usually the last thing on the packet you read: parthenocarpic. The only variety that sets fruit on an indoor shelf is a parthenocarpic one. Not gynoecious. Not burpless. Not "no bees required" or "self pollinating." Parthenocarpic, spelled out, in the variety description. If the listing does not say it, assume the packet needs pollen and your spare room does not have any.</p>

<p>I stopped guessing and ran a test on my own shelf this spring. Three packets, two plants each, six plants total, one 40 dollar light, one feed, one room. The cheap hardware-store packet sold as "burpless mini" gave me zero cucumbers. The packet of Diva that every beginner list name-drops gave me one cucumber, then dropped every fruit after it. The Beit Alpha packet gave me 41 cucumbers over nine weeks.</p>

<p>That is 19 dollars and 95 cents of seed, one shelf, one season, and a 41 cucumber spread between the worst packet and the best. The difference was one word on the label. This article is how to read that label before you pay for the lesson.</p>

<h2 id="types">1. The 3 Flowering Types, and the Label Trap</h2>

<p>Cucumbers come in three flowering types, and every seed listing you will read is describing one of them badly. Here is the honest version, without the catalog poetry.</p>

