<p><strong>Here is the key insight:</strong> gynoecious and parthenocarpic are not the same trait, and the industry sells them as if they were. Gynoecious describes which flowers the plant makes. Parthenocarpic describes whether those flowers need pollen to finish a fruit. A gynoecious cucumber covered in female flowers still aborts every one of them in a still room with nothing to move pollen.</p>

<p>Which brings us to the label trap. Seed racks and Amazon listings love the phrase "self pollinating." It is not a botanical category. It is a marketing phrase that gets pasted onto gynoecious varieties, onto monoecious varieties sold in small packets, and onto genuinely parthenocarpic ones. I have seen it on all three. The only word with a definition behind it is parthenocarpic.</p>

<p>Seed catalogs are better about this now, but not perfect. Fedco, Johnny's and High Mowing all list the flowering type on the variety page. The hardware store display rack does not. That is where my zero-cucumber packet came from, and the label gave me nothing to check.</p>

<h2 id="varieties">2. The Varieties That Actually Show Up in Grower Threads</h2>

<p>I read through the seed catalogs, then cross-checked every name against grower threads and university variety lists, because catalogs will describe a variety that nobody has managed to fruit. These are the eight names that keep appearing in all three places.</p>

<p><strong>Pay attention to this number:</strong> about 25 seeds per packet for the hybrids below, and a home grower needs one plant per 5 gallon container. That is roughly eight to ten plants of one variety from a single packet, or two packets for a season of three plants with room for a failed germination. A packet is not one plant. It is a season.</p>

